# ---- 686 Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science ----
H("686.d.1.A",3,"A programmer turns the rule 'if the door is open and the alarm is armed, then sound the siren' into P AND Q → R. This is:","modeling a real situation with symbolic logic",["measuring a circuit","drawing a flowchart by hand","sorting a list"],"Symbolic logic expresses real conditions with symbols and connectives.")
H("686.d.1.A",4,"Why do computer scientists translate real-world rules into symbolic logic before coding them?","Symbols remove ambiguity so the rule can be tested exactly",["Symbols run faster on all hardware","Logic replaces the need for testing","It makes the rule shorter in English"],"Formal symbols have one precise meaning.")
H("686.d.1.B",3,"A map of cities joined by roads is best modeled in computer science as a:","graph with vertices and edges",["stack of plates","single variable","sorted array only"],"Cities are vertices and roads are edges.")
H("686.d.1.B",4,"A company org chart where each manager has several direct reports and no loops is best modeled as a:","tree",["cycle graph","complete graph","truth table"],"A tree is a connected graph with no cycles.")
H("686.d.1.C",3,"A fair six-sided die is rolled once. The probability of rolling an even number is:","1/2",["1/6","1/3","2/3"],"Three of six outcomes are even.")
H("686.d.1.C",4,"A fair die is rolled. The expected value of the number shown is:","3.5",["3","4","6"],"(1+2+3+4+5+6)/6 = 3.5.")
H("686.d.2.A",3,"'It is raining or it is snowing' becomes which propositional form if P = raining and Q = snowing?","P ∨ Q",["P ∧ Q","¬P","P → Q"],"'Or' translates to disjunction.")
H("686.d.2.A",4,"'If I finish homework, then I can play' with P = finish homework and Q = play is written:","P → Q",["P ∨ Q","Q → ¬P","P ∧ ¬Q"],"'If…then' is implication.")
H("686.d.2.B",3,"In discrete math, a function from set A to set B assigns each element of A:","exactly one element of B",["no elements of B","every element of B","two elements of A"],"A function is single-valued.")
H("686.d.2.B",4,"A relation on a set differs from a function in that a relation may:","pair one input with several outputs",["use only numbers","have no inputs","never be written as ordered pairs"],"Relations are any sets of ordered pairs.")
H("686.d.2.C",3,"The Master theorem is used to solve:","divide-and-conquer recurrences like T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)",["truth tables","graph coloring puzzles","modular clock problems only"],"It gives running time of divide-and-conquer algorithms.")
H("686.d.2.C",4,"Merge sort has T(n)=2T(n/2)+n. By the Master theorem its time is:","Θ(n log n)",["Θ(n)","Θ(n²)","Θ(log n)"],"a=2, b=2, f(n)=n gives case 2: Θ(n log n).")
H("686.d.2.D",3,"A tool says a schedule graph needs 4 colors. In context, the number of colors means:","the number of time slots needed so no conflicts overlap",["the number of students","the file size","the number of bugs"],"Colors represent separate time slots.")
H("686.d.2.D",4,"In a social-network graph, a vertex with degree 120 means that person:","is directly linked to 120 others",["posted 120 times","is 120 years old","has 120 messages unread"],"Degree counts incident edges.")
H("686.d.2.E",3,"In set terms, A ∩ B means the elements that are:","in each of the two sets at once",["in the first set only","in the second set only","in exactly one of the two sets"],"Intersection means both sets.")
H("686.d.2.E",4,"The union of {1,2,3} and {3,4} is:","{1,2,3,4}",["{3}","{1,2,4}","{1,2,3,3,4}"],"Union combines elements without repeats.")
H("686.d.2.F",3,"Two statements that always have the same truth value are called:","logically equivalent",["contradictions","tautologies only","contingent"],"Equivalent statements match on every row of a truth table.")
H("686.d.2.F",4,"An argument is valid when:","the conclusion must be true whenever all premises are true",["the conclusion is surprising","the premises are false","it has two premises"],"Validity concerns structure, not truth of the premises.")
H("686.d.3.A",3,"In a truth table, P ∧ Q is true only when:","both P and Q are true",["either P or Q is true","both are false","P is false"],"Conjunction needs both.")
H("686.d.3.A",4,"The implication P → Q is false only when:","P is true and Q is false",["P is false and Q is true","both are true","both are false"],"A true premise leading to a false conclusion breaks the implication.")
H("686.d.3.B",3,"Two propositions whose truth-table columns match on every row are:","equivalent",["unrelated","contradictory","undefined"],"Identical columns show equivalence.")
H("686.d.3.B",4,"A truth table for (P ∨ ¬P) shows every row true, so the statement is a:","tautology",["contradiction","contingency","predicate"],"Always true means tautology.")
H("686.d.4.A",3,"A school database lists which student is in which club. This is best modeled with a:","relation between students and clubs",["single integer","loop counter","password"],"A relation pairs students with clubs.")
H("686.d.4.A",4,"Assigning each student ID to exactly one locker is best modeled as a:","function from IDs to lockers",["relation allowing many lockers per ID","set of lockers only","tree"],"Each input gets exactly one output.")
H("686.d.4.B",3,"A statement that is false on every row of its truth table is a:","contradiction",["tautology","contingency","theorem"],"Always false means contradiction.")
H("686.d.4.B",4,"A statement true on some rows and false on others is called a:","contingency",["tautology","contradiction","axiom"],"Its truth depends on the inputs.")
H("686.d.4.C",3,"The contrapositive of 'if it rains, the game is canceled' is:","if the game is not canceled, it did not rain",["if it does not rain, the game is not canceled","if the game is canceled, it rained","if it rains, the game is played"],"Contrapositive negates and swaps: ¬Q → ¬P.")
H("686.d.4.C",4,"To disprove 'every prime number is odd' you give a counterexample such as:","2",["3","5","7"],"2 is prime and even.")
H("686.d.4.D",3,"Predicate logic can say 'for every student x, x has an ID,' which propositional logic cannot easily express because it lacks:","quantifiers",["negation","truth values","variables of any kind"],"Quantifiers like ∀ and ∃ extend propositional logic.")
H("686.d.4.D",4,"A limitation of predicate logic reasoning is that, unlike a truth table, checking validity in general is:","undecidable by any single algorithm",["always trivial","impossible to write down","limited to two variables"],"First-order validity is only semi-decidable.")
H("686.d.4.E",3,"Applying De Morgan's law, ¬(P ∧ Q) is equivalent to:","¬P ∨ ¬Q",["¬P ∧ ¬Q","P ∨ Q","¬P → Q"],"Negating a conjunction flips to a disjunction of negations.")
H("686.d.4.E",4,"Using modus ponens, from 'P → Q' and 'P' you may conclude:","Q",["¬P","¬Q","P → ¬Q"],"Modus ponens affirms the antecedent.")
H("686.d.4.F",3,"A proof that a loop terminates uses logical reasoning to show:","the loop condition eventually becomes false",["the code is short","the loop is fast","the variable is a string"],"Termination proofs show the stopping condition is reached.")
H("686.d.4.F",4,"A rule of inference lets a prover:","derive a new true statement from earlier true ones",["guess a conclusion","delete a premise","change the problem"],"Inference rules preserve truth.")
H("686.d.4.G",3,"A direct proof of 'if n is even, then n² is even' starts by:","assuming n is even and showing n² is even",["assuming n² is odd","testing n = 2 only","assuming the conclusion is false"],"Direct proofs go from hypothesis to conclusion.")
H("686.d.4.G",4,"A proof by contradiction begins by assuming:","the statement to be proved is false",["the statement is true","a smaller case is true","the premises are false"],"A contradiction then shows the assumption impossible.")
H("686.d.4.H",3,"Proving 'if n² is even then n is even' is easiest by assuming n is odd. This is a:","proof by contraposition",["direct proof","proof by cases","induction"],"Assuming ¬Q and deriving ¬P is contrapositive.")
H("686.d.4.H",4,"Showing 'the square root of 2 is irrational' is classically done by:","contradiction",["a truth table","brute-force checking","counting arguments"],"Assume rational, then derive an impossibility.")
H("686.d.4.I",3,"A recursive function calls itself on a smaller input, and induction proves it correct by showing the:","base case and the step from k to k+1",["largest input only","output is a string","code compiles"],"Induction mirrors recursion's base and recursive steps.")
H("686.d.4.I",4,"A recursively defined list like f(0)=1, f(n)=2·f(n−1) is naturally verified using:","mathematical induction",["a Venn diagram","random sampling","a pigeonhole argument"],"Recursion and induction are closely linked.")
H("686.d.4.J",3,"Strong induction differs from weak induction in that the inductive step may assume:","all earlier cases are true",["only the next case","no cases","only the base case"],"Strong induction assumes P(1)…P(k).")
H("686.d.4.J",4,"Structural induction is the best fit for proving a property of:","recursively defined trees or expressions",["decimal numbers only","random samples","hardware speeds"],"It follows the recursive construction rules of a structure.")
H("686.d.4.K",3,"Flipping a coin twice, the second flip's outcome is:","independent of the first",["dependent on the first","always the same as the first","impossible to predict"],"Coin flips do not affect each other.")
H("686.d.4.K",4,"Drawing two cards from a deck without replacing the first makes the draws:","dependent events",["independent events","impossible events","certain events"],"The first draw changes the second's probabilities.")
H("686.d.4.L",3,"A program's time satisfies T(n)=T(n−1)+1 with T(1)=1. T(5) equals:","5",["4","6","10"],"Each step adds 1: 1,2,3,4,5.")
H("686.d.4.L",4,"The recurrence T(n)=2T(n−1)+1 with T(1)=1 gives T(4)=","15",["8","10","16"],"1, 3, 7, 15.")
H("686.d.4.M",3,"An algorithm tries every ordering of 4 items. The number of orderings it must check is:","24",["4","12","16"],"4! = 24.")
H("686.d.4.M",4,"A lock code uses 3 digits (0–9), repeats allowed. The number of possible codes is:","1000",["720","100","27"],"10×10×10 = 1000.")
H("686.d.4.N",3,"A bag has 3 red and 2 blue marbles. The probability of picking blue is:","2/5",["3/5","1/2","2/3"],"Two of five marbles are blue.")
H("686.d.4.N",4,"A fair coin is flipped 3 times. The probability of getting exactly 2 heads is:","3/8",["1/4","1/2","1/8"],"C(3,2)/8 = 3/8.")
H("686.d.4.O",3,"A simple graph differs from a multigraph because a simple graph has:","no parallel edges and no loops",["only one vertex","directed edges only","no vertices"],"Multigraphs allow repeated edges.")
H("686.d.4.O",4,"A road network with one-way streets should be modeled as a:","directed graph",["undirected graph","tree only","simple set"],"Edges have direction.")
H("686.d.5.A",3,"Using a photo from another creator in a class project is ethical when you:","have permission or a license and give credit",["remove the creator's name","assume anything online is free","crop out the watermark"],"Ethical use respects rights.")
H("686.d.5.A",4,"Copying a classmate's proof and submitting it as yours is:","plagiarism",["collaboration","citation","peer review"],"Presenting others' work as your own is plagiarism.")
H("686.d.5.B",3,"Proper digital etiquette in a class forum includes:","staying respectful and on topic",["posting in all capitals","sharing passwords","replying with insults"],"Etiquette keeps discussions productive.")
H("686.d.5.B",4,"Installing licensed software on one computer and then copying it to many without permission violates:","the software license",["a font rule","a color rule","a file name rule"],"Licenses limit how many installs are allowed.")
H("686.d.5.C",3,"Graph theory helps a delivery company by:","finding short routes between stops",["printing labels","adding colors","sorting fonts"],"Shortest-path ideas come from graphs.")
H("686.d.5.C",4,"Counting techniques such as combinations help a password security analyst estimate:","how many possible passwords an attacker must try",["monitor brightness","internet cost","keyboard layout"],"More possible passwords means more attacks needed.")
H("686.d.6.A",3,"If A={1,2,3} and B={3,4}, then A∪B has how many elements?","4",["2","3","5"],"{1,2,3,4} has 4 elements.")
H("686.d.6.A",4,"If A={1,2,3,4} and B={2,4,6}, then A−B is:","{1,3}",["{2,4}","{6}","{1,2,3,4,6}"],"A−B keeps elements in A not in B.")
H("686.d.6.B",3,"Eleven people share birthdays across 10 months. By the pigeonhole principle, at least two share:","a birth month",["a birth year","a first name","a height"],"More items than boxes forces a repeat.")
H("686.d.6.B",4,"The cardinality of the set {a, b, {c, d}} is:","3",["2","4","5"],"The set holds three elements; one is itself a set.")
H("686.d.6.C",3,"In most logic systems, which operator is evaluated first in P ∨ ¬Q?","¬",["∨","P","none of them"],"Negation has the highest precedence.")
H("686.d.6.C",4,"With standard precedence, P ∨ Q ∧ R is read as:","P ∨ (Q ∧ R)",["(P ∨ Q) ∧ R","(P ∨ Q) ∨ R","P ∧ (Q ∨ R)"],"AND binds tighter than OR.")
H("686.d.6.D",3,"De Morgan's law says ¬(P ∨ Q) is the same as:","¬P ∧ ¬Q",["¬P ∨ ¬Q","P ∧ Q","P → Q"],"Negating OR gives AND of negations.")
H("686.d.6.D",4,"To show P → Q is equivalent to ¬P ∨ Q, a student can:","compare their truth tables",["count variables","factor an integer","draw a tree"],"Matching truth tables prove equivalence.")
H("686.d.6.E",3,"The symbol ∃ in predicate logic means:","there exists",["for all","not","implies"],"∃ is the existential quantifier.")
H("686.d.6.E",4,"'Every student has a locker' is best written with the quantifier:","∀",["∃","¬","↔"],"∀ is the universal quantifier.")
H("686.d.6.F",3,"A proof of algorithm complexity shows how an algorithm's work:","grows as the input grows",["looks on screen","is colored","is named"],"Complexity proofs bound growth.")
H("686.d.6.F",4,"A loop invariant is used in a proof to show:","a condition stays true each time through the loop",["the loop never runs","the code is short","the variable is private"],"Invariants support correctness proofs.")
H("686.d.6.G",3,"A shirt comes in 4 colors and 3 sizes. By the product rule, the number of kinds is:","12",["7","9","16"],"4×3 = 12.")
H("686.d.6.G",4,"You can take a bus (5 routes) or a train (3 routes) but not both. By the sum rule, the choices total:","8",["15","2","53"],"5+3 = 8.")
H("686.d.6.H",3,"The number of ways to arrange 3 different books on a shelf is:","6",["3","9","27"],"3! = 6.")
H("686.d.6.H",4,"Choosing 2 team captains from 5 players, order not mattering, can be done in:","10 ways",["20 ways","25 ways","5 ways"],"C(5,2) = 10.")
H("686.d.6.I",3,"If a(1)=3 and a(n)=a(n−1)+2, then a(4) is:","9",["5","7","11"],"3, 5, 7, 9.")
H("686.d.6.I",4,"If a(1)=1 and a(n)=3·a(n−1), then a(4) is:","27",["9","12","81"],"1, 3, 9, 27.")
H("686.d.6.J",3,"The binomial theorem expands (x + y)² as:","x² + 2xy + y²",["x² + y²","2x + 2y","x² − y²"],"Coefficients come from the binomial row 1 2 1.")
H("686.d.6.J",4,"How many ways can exactly 2 successes occur in 4 independent trials (count only)?","6",["4","8","12"],"C(4,2) = 6.")
H("686.d.6.K",3,"Bayes' theorem is used to:","update a probability after new evidence",["sort a list","draw a graph","compress a file"],"It reverses conditional probabilities.")
H("686.d.6.K",4,"A spam filter flags words and then uses Bayes' theorem to estimate:","the chance a message is spam given its words",["the sender's age","the message length in bytes","the server speed"],"It computes P(spam | words).")
H("686.d.6.L",3,"A traversal that visits a tree's left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree is called:","in-order",["pre-order","post-order","level-order"],"Left, node, right is in-order.")
H("686.d.6.L",4,"A traversal that explores a graph by visiting all neighbors before moving deeper is:","breadth-first search",["depth-first search","binary search","hash search"],"BFS expands level by level.")
H("686.d.6.M",3,"Which data structure naturally stores a family tree?","a tree",["a stack of numbers","a single integer","a bit flag"],"Parent–child relationships form a tree.")
H("686.d.6.M",4,"A graph stored as a list of each vertex's neighbors is called an:","adjacency list",["index card","hash digest","binary heap"],"Adjacency lists are compact for sparse graphs.")

# ---- 687 Game Programming and Design ----
H("687.d.1.A",3,"The story, characters and goals a designer writes for a game form its:","narrative and concept",["file format","network speed","power supply"],"Storyline and concepts shape design.")
H("687.d.1.A",4,"A designer who decides the player must rescue a lost robot across three worlds is working on the game's:","storyline and objectives",["audio compression","memory limits","server cost"],"Objectives and story are core design elements.")
H("687.d.1.B",3,"A design concept document for a game is used to:","describe the game idea, rules and goals for the team",["install the game","sell the console","test the sound card"],"It communicates the plan.")
H("687.d.1.B",4,"A concept document usually includes the game's genre, target players and:","core gameplay and goals",["the printer model","student passwords","wiring diagrams"],"Core play and goals define the game.")
H("687.d.1.C",3,"A game storyboard shows:","key scenes or screens in order, like a comic strip",["the final source code","the sales numbers","the file sizes"],"Storyboards plan visual flow.")
H("687.d.1.C",4,"A storyboard helps a team catch a confusing level layout:","before any expensive coding is done",["after launch only","never","only by testing audio"],"Drawing first is cheap to change.")
H("687.d.1.D",3,"The overall 'look' of a game's art, such as pixel art or cartoon, is its:","art style",["compiler","frame loop","port number"],"Style sets a consistent look.")
H("687.d.1.D",4,"Keeping a character's colors and proportions the same across all levels shows a focus on:","visual consistency",["audio mixing","server load","file naming only"],"Consistency makes art feel unified.")
H("687.d.1.E",3,"A bitmap image is made of:","a grid of colored pixels",["mathematical curves","text characters only","sound waves"],"Bitmaps store pixels.")
H("687.d.1.E",4,"Enlarging a small bitmap sprite a lot usually makes it look:","blocky or blurry",["sharper","smaller","louder"],"Bitmaps lose clarity when scaled up.")
H("687.d.1.F",3,"A tiled background repeats one small image to cover a larger area. This saves:","memory and drawing effort",["story ideas","sound files","player names"],"One tile can fill the screen.")
H("687.d.1.F",4,"A solid-color background differs from an image background because it:","uses a single fill color with no picture",["needs a sprite sheet","requires audio","must be animated"],"A solid background is one color.")
H("687.d.1.G",3,"To draw a rectangle in code, a program typically needs its position and:","width and height",["a song title","a password","a file extension"],"Rectangles need size and location.")
H("687.d.1.G",4,"A circle is usually drawn from a center point and a:","radius",["tile map","soundtrack","hash"],"Center and radius define a circle.")
H("687.d.1.H",3,"A sprite in a game is:","a 2D image or animation that represents an object",["a type of loop","a server","a rule document"],"Sprites are movable game images.")
H("687.d.1.H",4,"To make a sprite walk, a developer changes its position and also:","swaps its animation frames",["deletes its image","raises the volume","changes the story"],"Frame changes create motion.")
H("687.d.1.I",3,"A sprite sheet is one image that holds:","many frames of a character's animation",["only background music","the game's source code","the player's score"],"Sheets pack frames together.")
H("687.d.1.I",4,"Using a sprite sheet instead of many separate image files usually:","reduces loading overhead",["raises memory errors","removes animation","prevents scaling"],"One file loads faster than many.")
H("687.d.1.J",3,"Transparency in a sprite lets the:","background show through parts of the image",["sprite play sound","screen go dark","code run faster"],"Transparent pixels reveal what's behind.")
H("687.d.1.J",4,"The number of times per second a game redraws its screen is its:","refresh rate (frame rate)",["bit depth","port","file path"],"Refresh rate measures redraws per second.")
H("687.d.1.K",3,"A short sound played when a player collects a coin is a:","sound effect",["texture","tile","variable"],"Effects give action feedback.")
H("687.d.1.K",4,"Editing a sound file to remove silence at the start helps a game by:","making the sound play at the right moment",["adding colors","saving a sprite","creating a level"],"Silence causes audible delay.")
H("687.d.1.L",3,"Looping background music means it:","repeats continuously",["plays only once","never starts","is deleted"],"Looping restarts the track.")
H("687.d.1.L",4,"A pause button in a game should also:","pause or lower the game audio",["delete the save file","increase the score","reset the level"],"Pausing audio matches the paused state.")
H("687.d.2.A",3,"Setting deadlines for art, code and testing and checking them weekly is:","project timeline tracking",["sprite editing","audio looping","pixel counting"],"Timelines keep a project on schedule.")
H("687.d.2.A",4,"A team that finds its level design is two weeks behind should:","adjust the schedule or scope and tell the team",["hide the delay","stop testing","delete the level"],"Tracking exists to guide adjustments.")
H("687.d.2.B",3,"Asking classmates to play a prototype and tell you what confused them is:","gathering peer feedback",["debugging a sprite","compressing audio","writing a license"],"Feedback shows problems you missed.")
H("687.d.2.B",4,"When five testers all get stuck at the same jump, the designer should:","redesign that part of the level",["ignore it","blame the testers","remove the sound"],"Repeated problems signal a design flaw.")
H("687.d.2.C",3,"A game developer uses an integrated development environment (IDE) mainly to:","write, run and debug code in one place",["print posters","record voice calls","browse social media"],"IDEs bundle coding tools.")
H("687.d.2.C",4,"Version control software helps a game team by:","tracking changes and letting members merge work safely",["raising the frame rate","creating sprites automatically","compressing music"],"It records history and avoids overwrites.")
H("687.d.2.D",3,"Using a school network to find a free-to-use sound library is an example of:","using network resources to acquire information",["editing sprites","coding a loop","drawing a storyboard"],"Networks give access to resources.")
H("687.d.2.D",4,"A team keeps assets in labeled folders on a shared drive to:","organize and maintain files so anyone can find them",["hide files from teammates","increase file size","avoid backups"],"Good organization saves time.")
H("687.d.2.E",3,"In the game business, a publisher typically:","funds, markets and distributes games",["draws every sprite","writes every line of code","plays testers' games"],"Publishers handle money and distribution.")
H("687.d.2.E",4,"A small team that designs and programs its own game and sells it directly is an:","independent (indie) developer",["publisher only","retailer only","console maker"],"Indies create and often self-publish.")
H("687.d.2.F",3,"Playing with friends over the internet in the same game world is called:","online multiplayer",["offline solitaire","a screensaver","a spreadsheet"],"Online play connects players over a network.")
H("687.d.2.F",4,"One challenge of online games compared to offline ones is:","lag caused by network delay",["no sound","no graphics","no rules"],"Latency affects online play.")
H("687.d.3.A",3,"Watching people play a board game and noting what slows them down is:","collecting game play data",["compiling code","editing audio","drawing sprites"],"Observation is research.")
H("687.d.3.A",4,"Timing how long each turn takes in a board game helps a designer see:","where the pacing drags",["what font to use","how loud to be","which console to use"],"Turn times reveal pacing issues.")
H("687.d.3.B",3,"Playability describes how easy and enjoyable a game is to:","learn and play",["download","advertise","store"],"Playability measures player experience.")
H("687.d.3.B",4,"A tester writes down that the tutorial confuses new players. This note is:","documentation of a playability issue",["a sprite sheet","a license","a loop"],"Documented findings guide revisions.")
H("687.d.3.C",3,"Different players enjoy different things, such as winning, exploring or socializing. These are called:","player types",["file types","data types","loop types"],"Player types describe motivations.")
H("687.d.3.C",4,"A game with big maps and hidden secrets mostly appeals to which kind of player?","explorers",["speedrunners only","spectators only","none"],"Explorers enjoy discovery.")
H("687.d.4.A",3,"The game design process often begins with:","generating and sorting ideas",["selling the game","shipping the disc","locking the code"],"Ideas come first, then prototypes.")
H("687.d.4.A",4,"After choosing an idea, a designer commonly builds a quick:","prototype to test the core mechanic",["final soundtrack","marketing poster","box design"],"Prototypes test ideas early.")
H("687.d.4.B",3,"A variable that stores a player's score as a whole number uses the data type:","integer",["string","boolean","image"],"Whole numbers are integers.")
H("687.d.4.B",4,"A variable that is either true or false, such as isGameOver, has the type:","boolean",["float","string","array"],"Booleans hold true/false.")
H("687.d.4.C",3,"Good game instructions should be:","clear and short enough to follow quickly",["secret","hidden in code","written in a rare language"],"Players need understandable rules.")
H("687.d.4.C",4,"If playtesters keep misreading the rules, the rules probably need to be:","reworded or illustrated",["removed entirely","made longer and vaguer","printed smaller"],"Revise unclear rules.")
H("687.d.4.D",3,"The user experience of a game is how it feels to the:","person playing it",["compiler","server","publisher's accountant"],"UX is the player's experience.")
H("687.d.4.D",4,"If the rules say 'press A to jump' but the game uses the space bar, the mismatch hurts:","the user experience",["the frame rate","the file size","the sprite colors"],"Rules and controls must agree.")
H("687.d.4.E",3,"Writing game rules that a new player can follow without help requires:","simple, step-by-step wording",["advanced jargon","long essays","random order"],"Simple steps aid understanding.")
H("687.d.4.E",4,"Rules that list the goal, how to move and how to win or lose are:","complete rules",["unfinished rules","art notes","debug logs"],"Good rules cover goal, actions and outcomes.")
H("687.d.4.F",3,"Game software is built by combining code, art, audio and:","game rules and logic",["printer drivers","spreadsheets","email"],"Logic ties the assets together.")
H("687.d.4.F",4,"Developing in small working pieces and testing each is called:","iterative development",["one-shot printing","random coding","cold storage"],"Iteration finds problems early.")
H("687.d.4.G",3,"A bug where the player falls through the floor is a game:","defect",["feature","sprite","theme"],"Defects are unintended faults.")
H("687.d.4.G",4,"After fixing a defect, a developer should:","retest to be sure it is fixed and nothing else broke",["delete the test","skip testing","hide the bug"],"Verification prevents regressions.")
H("687.d.4.H",3,"Testing a game in a planned, repeatable way is:","sound testing practice",["guesswork","avoidance","luck"],"Planned tests find more faults.")
H("687.d.4.H",4,"Playing only the easy first level before release is poor testing because it:","misses problems in harder or later parts",["tests everything","finds all bugs","is too thorough"],"Coverage matters.")
H("687.d.5.A",3,"Copyright law protects a game's art, code and music from:","being copied without permission",["being played","being sold legally","being reviewed"],"Copyright limits unauthorized copying.")
H("687.d.5.A",4,"A player's data like a username and email in a game raises concerns about:","privacy",["frame rate","art style","level design"],"Personal data needs protection.")
H("687.d.5.B",3,"Using an artist's character in your game ethically requires:","permission or a license",["a new name only","hiding the source","a lower price"],"Respect others' creations.")
H("687.d.5.B",4,"Using free, openly licensed music in a game is ethical when you:","follow the license terms, such as crediting the author",["ignore the terms","claim you wrote it","resell the track"],"Licenses set conditions.")
H("687.d.5.C",3,"Proper digital etiquette in an online game chat means:","treating other players respectfully",["spamming","sharing passwords","insulting teammates"],"Etiquette keeps spaces safe.")
H("687.d.5.C",4,"An acceptable use policy at school says that game devices should be used:","only as the policy allows",["any way you like","to bypass filters","to harass others"],"AUPs set rules.")
H("687.d.5.D",3,"Morphing or editing a copyrighted picture does not by itself:","remove the original owner's rights",["change the file type","make it brighter","shrink it"],"Altering doesn't erase copyright.")
H("687.d.5.D",4,"To safely include a recognizable character's image in a school project, a student should:","get permission or use original art",["trace it closely and say it is original","copy it silently","change one color only"],"Original or permitted art is safe.")
H("687.d.5.E",3,"One social issue discussed about gaming is:","how much time players spend playing",["font choice","the color red","tile sizes"],"Screen time is a common concern.")
H("687.d.5.E",4,"A positive social effect of gaming can be:","teamwork and problem-solving skills",["lower grades guaranteed","isolation guaranteed","no effect ever"],"Games can build collaboration.")
H("687.d.5.F",3,"A game set in ancient Egypt should be designed with:","respect for the culture it portrays",["random symbols","no research","mocking stereotypes"],"Cultural accuracy shows care.")
H("687.d.5.F",4,"Designers consult cultural advisers so their game avoids:","harmful stereotypes and errors",["all colors","all audio","all sprites"],"Advisers help portray cultures fairly.")
H("687.d.6.A",3,"The part of a game that runs the main loop, draws graphics and handles input is the:","game engine",["sprite sheet","storyboard","rule book"],"Engines provide core systems.")
H("687.d.6.A",4,"Game play subsystems include things like physics, input handling and:","rendering",["tax filing","email","accounting"],"Rendering draws the scene.")
H("687.d.6.B",3,"A game uses a random number to decide which enemy appears. Using it makes play:","less predictable",["identical each time","impossible","silent"],"Randomness adds variety.")
H("687.d.6.B",4,"To roll a die from 1 to 6 with a function that returns 0–5, you can:","add 1 to the result",["subtract 1","double it","square it"],"Shifts 0–5 to 1–6.")
H("687.d.6.C",3,"A conditional statement lets a game:","do something only if a condition is true",["run forever","draw sprites only","play music only"],"Conditionals branch logic.")
H("687.d.6.C",4,"Code that says 'if lives == 0, show Game Over' is an example of a:","conditional",["loop","array","class"],"It acts only when a condition holds.")
H("687.d.6.D",3,"A data model for a game player might store name, health and:","inventory",["wallpaper","the printer","the weather"],"The model holds the player's data.")
H("687.d.6.D",4,"Keeping player data in one organized structure makes it easier to:","save, load and update",["hide it","lose it","duplicate bugs"],"Good models simplify handling.")
H("687.d.6.E",3,"In object-oriented game programming, a class is like a:","blueprint for creating game objects",["screen","speaker","cable"],"Classes define object templates.")
H("687.d.6.E",4,"If Enemy is a class, then each individual goblin in the game is:","an object (instance)",["a class","a loop","a file"],"Instances come from classes.")
H("687.d.6.F",3,"Event-driven programming means the program reacts to:","things like key presses and clicks",["only the clock","only code comments","nothing"],"Events trigger responses.")
H("687.d.6.F",4,"A game that waits for the player to press a key before moving the hero is using:","event-driven design",["batch processing","a static page","a screensaver"],"Input events drive actions.")
H("687.d.6.G",3,"A game's animation loop repeatedly:","updates and redraws the scene",["deletes the sprites","closes the game","prints the code"],"The loop produces motion.")
H("687.d.6.G",4,"If the loop runs too slowly, the player will see:","choppy motion",["better graphics","faster music","longer levels"],"Low frame rate looks choppy.")
H("687.d.6.H",3,"Using an existing game engine can save time because it:","provides ready-made tools for graphics and input",["writes the story","plays the game","sells the game"],"Engines supply common functions.")
H("687.d.6.H",4,"A team chooses an engine partly based on the platforms it supports and its:","learning curve and cost",["color","slogan","box art"],"Fit and cost matter.")
H("687.d.6.I",3,"Video display flicker happens when a screen is:","redrawn in a way the eye can notice",["too bright","too small","off"],"Visible redraws look like flicker.")
H("687.d.6.I",4,"Double buffering reduces flicker by drawing to:","an off-screen image first, then showing it at once",["the speakers","the keyboard","the network"],"The finished frame is swapped in.")
H("687.d.6.J",3,"A game's heads-up display (HUD) shows the player things like:","score and health",["server logs","source code","sales data"],"HUDs show key status.")
H("687.d.6.J",4,"A well-laid-out screen puts the most important information where it is:","easy to see without blocking play",["hidden","tiny","off screen"],"Layout supports readability.")
H("687.d.6.K",3,"A game controller, keyboard and mouse are all:","input devices",["output devices only","storage devices","power devices"],"They send player input.")
H("687.d.6.K",4,"A game that supports both touch and gamepad controls must:","handle input from each type of device",["ignore both","use only one","remove the input"],"Code must read each device.")
H("687.d.6.L",3,"Squash and stretch, from animation principles, make movement feel more:","lively and natural",["silent","flat","random"],"These principles add life.")
H("687.d.6.L",4,"Easing a character's start and stop so it speeds up and slows down is called:","slow in and slow out",["hard cut","pixel swap","tile drop"],"Easing mimics real motion.")
H("687.d.6.M",3,"A game chooses what an enemy does based on the player's distance. This is:","decision making in code",["sound design","level art","packaging"],"Decisions use conditions.")
H("687.d.6.M",4,"A decision with only two outcomes, like attack or flee, is:","a binary decision",["a continuous decision","a random decision","an art decision"],"Two outcomes make a binary choice.")
H("687.d.6.N",3,"A trigger in a game is a zone that:","starts an action when the player enters",["deletes the player","pauses the engine","prints a score"],"Triggers fire events.")
H("687.d.6.N",4,"A listener in event handling:","waits for a specific event and then runs code",["draws the sprites","plays the music loop","stores the score"],"Listeners respond to events.")
H("687.d.6.O",3,"Collision detection tells the game when:","two objects touch or overlap",["a song ends","a file saves","a level loads"],"Collisions trigger reactions.")
H("687.d.6.O",4,"Checking collisions using rectangles drawn around sprites is called:","bounding-box collision",["pixel painting","tile shuffling","texture baking"],"Boxes make fast checks.")
H("687.d.6.P",3,"A tile-based game builds its world from:","small repeating square images",["one huge photograph","only text","only sound"],"Tiles tile the map.")
H("687.d.6.P",4,"A tile map is often stored as:","a grid of numbers pointing to tile images",["a movie","a sound clip","a password"],"Numbers index tiles.")
H("687.d.6.Q",3,"Artificial intelligence in a game controls how:","non-player characters make decisions",["the disc spins","the logo looks","the box prints"],"AI drives NPC behavior.")
H("687.d.6.Q",4,"A simple AI that moves an enemy toward the player each turn is using:","chase behavior",["random music","menu logic","save slots"],"Chasing follows the target.")
H("687.d.6.R",3,"Game balance means that no strategy or character is:","unfairly stronger than all the others",["visible","colorful","loud"],"Balance keeps play fair.")
H("687.d.6.R",4,"If everyone picks the same overpowered character, the designer should:","tune the character's stats",["delete the menu","add more music","ignore it"],"Tuning adjusts values.")
H("687.d.6.S",3,"Leveling up in a game is an example of:","player progression",["file compression","network lag","screen flicker"],"Progression rewards advancement.")
H("687.d.6.S",4,"A game where levels must be finished in one fixed order uses:","linear progression",["open-world progression only","no progression","random progression"],"Linear games run in order.")

# ---- 688 Mobile Application Development ----
H("688.d.1.A",3,"A phone app's buttons should be large enough for fingers, which makes the interface suited to its:","touch screen",["keyboard only","mouse wheel","printer"],"Touch targets must be finger-sized.")
H("688.d.1.A",4,"A tablet app often shows more content per screen than a phone app because the tablet has:","a larger display",["a slower processor","fewer pixels","no battery"],"Screen size changes layout.")
H("688.d.1.B",3,"A browser-based mobile app runs inside the phone's:","web browser",["camera","speaker","charger"],"Web apps use the browser.")
H("688.d.1.B",4,"A hybrid mobile app combines web technology with:","a native app wrapper",["a printer driver","a modem","a spreadsheet"],"Hybrids wrap web code in a native shell.")
H("688.d.1.C",3,"A login screen, a menu and a map view are each a:","component of the app",["network cable","battery","firmware"],"Components are building blocks.")
H("688.d.1.C",4,"Before building a component, a developer should confirm it meets:","an identified user need",["a friend's opinion only","a random guess","the last app's color"],"Needs drive components.")
H("688.d.1.D",3,"Browser-based mobile apps are built mainly with HTML, CSS and:","JavaScript",["assembly only","COBOL","Fortran"],"Web technologies power browser apps.")
H("688.d.1.D",4,"One advantage of a browser-based app is that users do not need to:","install it from an app store",["use the internet","use a screen","own a phone"],"Web apps open through a link.")
H("688.d.1.E",3,"A native app is built for:","a specific mobile platform such as iOS or Android",["every device without changes","only desktops","only printers"],"Native apps target one platform.")
H("688.d.1.E",4,"A native app can often use device features such as the camera and GPS more:","directly and efficiently",["slowly","never","only with a browser"],"Native code has direct access.")
H("688.d.1.F",3,"An app that has a native shell and web-based screens is built as a:","hybrid app",["pure native app","printed app","hardware app"],"Hybrid mixes both.")
H("688.d.1.F",4,"A reason to choose a hybrid approach is to:","reuse most code on several platforms",["avoid all testing","skip design","remove updates"],"Hybrids share code across platforms.")
H("688.d.2.A",3,"A team functions best when each member knows their:","role and responsibilities",["password only","phone brand","favorite color"],"Clear roles prevent confusion.")
H("688.d.2.A",4,"A team in which members share updates regularly is practicing good:","communication",["compression","encryption","formatting"],"Communication keeps work aligned.")
H("688.d.2.B",3,"A bug nobody can fix alone gets solved when the team:","shares ideas and tries solutions together",["quits","blames one person","ignores it"],"Teamwork solves problems.")
H("688.d.2.B",4,"Pair programming, where two developers work at one computer, helps by:","catching errors early",["doubling battery life","removing testing","hiding code"],"Two sets of eyes spot mistakes.")
H("688.d.2.C",3,"A typical development workflow for a mobile app moves from planning to design, then to coding and:","testing",["selling","deleting","guessing"],"Testing follows building.")
H("688.d.2.C",4,"After release, a team continues the workflow with:","updates and maintenance",["deleting the source","hiding the app","closing the team"],"Apps need ongoing care.")
H("688.d.2.D",3,"Breaking a big app into small tasks with due dates is a:","time-management technique",["compression method","coding language","hardware spec"],"Small deadlines keep progress steady.")
H("688.d.2.D",4,"A developer who updates a task board daily is practicing:","tracking progress against a schedule",["avoiding the team","skipping testing","ignoring deadlines"],"Tracking helps meet deadlines.")
H("688.d.2.E",3,"Writing 'users forget their passwords, so the app will include a reset link' describes a:","problem and its solution",["bug report only","logo","price"],"A problem statement paired with a fix.")
H("688.d.2.E",4,"A clear problem solution description tells what the problem is and:","how the app will fix it",["who owns the phone","what color to use","where the team eats"],"Solutions must be explained.")
H("688.d.2.F",3,"A developer records how an issue was fixed in a shared wiki so others can:","reuse the solution later",["forget it","delete it","hide it"],"Documentation spreads knowledge.")
H("688.d.2.F",4,"Sharing a solution through a short video demo is an example of using:","different media to communicate",["only text","only code","only email"],"Media choice suits the audience.")
H("688.d.3.A",3,"People who use, pay for or are affected by an app are its:","stakeholders",["compilers","firewalls","simulators"],"Stakeholders have an interest in the project.")
H("688.d.3.A",4,"A hospital's nurses, patients and IT staff are all stakeholders in a new patient-check-in app because each:","is affected by or involved with it",["owns the phone","designs the logo","writes the code"],"Different groups have different needs.")
H("688.d.3.B",3,"Surveys and interviews with users help a team find the app's:","requirements",["bugs only","icons only","fonts only"],"User data reveals needs.")
H("688.d.3.B",4,"If 80% of surveyed riders want offline maps, the team should list offline maps as:","a likely requirement",["an optional joke","a bug","a legal matter"],"High demand suggests a requirement.")
H("688.d.3.C",3,"For a calculator app, the numbers a user taps are the app's:","input",["output","storage","network"],"Typed numbers are input.")
H("688.d.3.C",4,"The processing step of a tip calculator app is:","multiplying the bill by the tip percent",["tapping the screen","showing the result","charging the phone"],"Processing transforms input to output.")
H("688.d.3.D",3,"Specifying that an app needs a camera and runs on Android 12 or later defines its:","hardware and software requirements",["story","logo","slogan"],"These are specifications.")
H("688.d.3.D",4,"Deciding how much memory and storage an app needs is part of defining:","hardware specifications",["marketing copy","logo design","team names"],"Memory and storage are hardware needs.")
H("688.d.4.A",3,"An app meant to be used one-handed while walking should have its main buttons:","within thumb reach",["at the top corner only","tiny","hidden in menus"],"Hardware size shapes design.")
H("688.d.4.A",4,"A phone's small screen and limited battery lead designers to choose:","simple layouts and efficient code",["huge animations","constant GPS","maximum brightness"],"Hardware limits shape decisions.")
H("688.d.4.B",3,"Two major mobile platforms are:","iOS and Android",["Excel and Word","Chrome and Edge","HTML and CSS"],"They are the main mobile operating systems.")
H("688.d.4.B",4,"A platform's app store rules affect developers by setting:","requirements for publishing apps",["the phone's color","the user's name","the weather"],"Stores enforce policies.")
H("688.d.4.C",3,"A developer who builds the app once in one codebase for multiple platforms is using a:","cross-platform approach",["single-device approach","paper approach","hardware approach"],"One codebase serves many platforms.")
H("688.d.4.C",4,"Building a separate app for each platform generally costs:","more time and effort",["less time and effort","no time","no effort"],"Separate code means duplicate work.")
H("688.d.4.D",3,"An app that must use advanced phone sensors at top speed is best built as:","a native app",["a web page only","a text file","a spreadsheet"],"Native code fits demanding tasks.")
H("688.d.4.D",4,"A simple info app for an event that must reach every device quickly could be:","a browser-based app",["a custom hardware device","a native-only app","an offline game"],"Web apps reach all devices easily.")
H("688.d.4.E",3,"Swift is a programming language used mainly for apps on:","Apple devices",["Android only","printers","fridges"],"Swift targets Apple platforms.")
H("688.d.4.E",4,"Kotlin and Java are commonly used to write apps for:","Android",["iPhone only","game consoles only","TVs only"],"They are Android languages.")
H("688.d.4.F",3,"A team skilled in JavaScript might pick a framework that uses:","JavaScript for the app",["only Fortran","only assembly","only COBOL"],"Skills influence language choice.")
H("688.d.4.F",4,"A developer chooses a language partly for its available libraries, community and:","platform support",["name length","logo","font"],"Support matters.")
H("688.d.4.G",3,"An IDE like Android Studio provides a code editor, an emulator and:","debugging tools",["a camera","a printer","a router"],"IDEs bundle tools.")
H("688.d.4.G",4,"Choosing a development environment depends on the target platform and the team's:","tools and experience",["lunch plans","desk size","shoe size"],"Familiar tools speed work.")
H("688.d.4.H",3,"A library in programming is:","reusable code that provides common functions",["a place with books","a phone model","a virus"],"Libraries save work.")
H("688.d.4.H",4,"Instead of writing a map from scratch, a developer can use a mapping library to:","add maps in less time",["delete maps","slow the app","remove GPS"],"Libraries speed development.")
H("688.d.4.I",3,"Picking the right components for an app means weighing needs, cost and:","performance",["spelling only","paper size","desk color"],"Evaluation guides choices.")
H("688.d.4.I",4,"Justifying a choice means giving:","reasons supported by requirements",["a coin toss","only an opinion","no explanation"],"Justification uses evidence.")
H("688.d.4.J",3,"An app that streams video works best on a:","fast, stable connection",["dead connection","no network","very slow connection"],"Bandwidth matters.")
H("688.d.4.J",4,"Cellular data differs from Wi-Fi in that cellular usually has:","data limits and variable speed",["no signal anywhere","unlimited speed always","no cost ever"],"Cellular plans cap data.")
H("688.d.4.K",3,"Encrypting data stored on a phone protects it if the phone is:","lost or stolen",["updated","charged","restarted"],"Encryption protects at rest.")
H("688.d.4.K",4,"Using secure connections like HTTPS in an app prevents:","eavesdropping on data in transit",["screen rotation","battery use","app updates"],"HTTPS encrypts traffic.")
H("688.d.5.A",3,"Copyright law gives a mobile app's creator the right to control:","who may copy and sell the app",["the user's phone color","the weather","the app store hours"],"Copyright protects creators' works.")
H("688.d.5.A",4,"Copying another developer's source code into your app without permission can lead to:","legal action for infringement",["a bonus","a free license","faster loading"],"Unlicensed copying infringes.")
H("688.d.5.B",3,"Using a free icon pack ethically means following its:","license terms",["color scheme only","file size only","name length"],"Licenses set conditions.")
H("688.d.5.B",4,"A developer who reads a user's contacts without clear consent is breaking:","ethical and privacy rules",["no rules","only a style rule","a naming rule"],"Data access needs consent.")
H("688.d.5.C",3,"When you use a code snippet from a tutorial in your project notes, you should:","credit the source",["claim it is yours","hide the link","delete the author"],"Citing gives credit.")
H("688.d.5.C",4,"An established way to list a source includes the author, title, date and:","where it can be found",["the phone number of the author","its file size","its color"],"Citations let others find sources.")
H("688.d.5.D",3,"An acceptable use policy for school devices usually forbids:","installing unapproved apps",["doing homework","using the calculator","reading class notes"],"AUPs limit risky actions.")
H("688.d.5.D",4,"Using a classroom tablet to bully a classmate violates both etiquette and:","the acceptable use policy",["the battery","the screen","the camera lens"],"Policies address misuse.")
H("688.d.5.E",3,"A passcode on a phone helps protect data by:","blocking access by unauthorized people",["speeding up the phone","adding storage","dimming the screen"],"Passcodes control access.")
H("688.d.5.E",4,"Antivirus software on a mobile device helps by:","detecting and removing malicious apps",["raising the volume","adding photos","changing wallpaper"],"Virus detection finds harmful code.")
H("688.d.5.F",3,"A benefit of a fitness tracking app is that it can:","help users monitor their activity",["replace doctors","cure illness","remove the need for sleep"],"Tracking supports habits.")
H("688.d.5.F",4,"A risk of a location-sharing app is that it may:","reveal where a user is",["lower prices","raise battery life","shrink the screen"],"Location data is sensitive.")
H("688.d.5.G",3,"Augmented reality apps place digital objects onto the:","live camera view of the real world",["printed page only","hard drive","modem"],"AR overlays the real scene.")
H("688.d.5.G",4,"A newer technology that lets phones pay at a store by tapping is:","near-field communication (NFC)",["a floppy disk","a fax","a radio dial"],"NFC enables tap payments.")
H("688.d.5.H",3,"Before adopting a new technology for an app, a team should assess:","whether it fits the app's needs",["its popularity only","its logo","its name"],"Fit comes first.")
H("688.d.5.H",4,"A team compares two new tools by testing each on a small feature. This is:","evaluating applicability with evidence",["guessing","copying","ignoring"],"Testing gives evidence.")
H("688.d.6.A",3,"Compared with desktop applications, mobile applications must work with:","smaller screens and touch input",["larger power supplies","unlimited memory","keyboards only"],"Mobile constraints differ.")
H("688.d.6.A",4,"A desktop app with tiny menus is hard to use on a phone because phone users:","tap with fingers on a small screen",["use bigger monitors","type 100 words per minute","use two mice"],"Touch needs bigger targets.")
H("688.d.6.B",3,"A mobile app needs enough device storage and an operating system version that:","supports its features",["is the oldest","is unknown","is blank"],"Apps depend on OS and hardware.")
H("688.d.6.B",4,"An app that uses the gyroscope will not work on a phone that:","lacks that sensor",["has a case","is blue","has a sticker"],"Hardware must match.")
H("688.d.6.C",3,"A platform is the combination of a device's operating system and:","its development tools and rules",["its case","its screen protector","its charger brand"],"Platforms include OS and ecosystem.")
H("688.d.6.C",4,"Each platform has its own design guidelines, so an app may need:","different menus or buttons on each platform",["no buttons","no menus","the same code in every case"],"Guidelines differ.")
H("688.d.6.D",3,"Xcode, Android Studio and Visual Studio are examples of:","development environments",["games","web browsers","storage drives"],"They are IDEs.")
H("688.d.6.D",4,"A program development environment commonly includes an editor, a compiler and:","a debugger",["a printer","a router","a speaker"],"Debuggers find faults.")
H("688.d.6.E",3,"In event-based programming, tapping a button is an:","event the program responds to",["error message","variable name","network cable"],"Taps generate events.")
H("688.d.6.E",4,"Code that runs when the user swipes left is called an:","event handler",["file header","storage unit","sprite sheet"],"Handlers respond to events.")
H("688.d.6.F",3,"A mobile app that uses too much memory can:","slow down or be closed by the operating system",["charge the phone","fix itself","boost the signal"],"Memory limits are strict on phones.")
H("688.d.6.F",4,"To reduce memory use, a developer might release images that are no longer:","needed by the app",["pretty","colorful","large"],"Freeing unused data saves memory.")
H("688.d.6.G",3,"Because users move between networks, an app should handle:","connection loss gracefully",["only perfect Wi-Fi","only desktop screens","only large files"],"Mobility means unstable connections.")
H("688.d.6.G",4,"An app that saves work locally when offline and syncs later is designed for:","low or intermittent bandwidth",["unlimited bandwidth only","no users","wired desktops only"],"Offline support helps mobile use.")
H("688.d.6.H",3,"An app that needs location and a camera, such as a trail photo map, fits a mobile device because the device:","has built-in sensors and is carried everywhere",["is always plugged in","has a huge screen","has no battery"],"Mobile devices have sensors and portability.")
H("688.d.6.H",4,"A heavy video-editing suite with many windows is better suited to a:","desktop computer",["smartwatch","fitness band","pager"],"Large screens and power suit editing.")
H("688.d.6.I",3,"Using a library for charts in an app means the developer does not have to:","write chart code from scratch",["install the phone","charge the battery","buy a screen"],"Libraries supply tested code.")
H("688.d.6.I",4,"When choosing a library, a developer should check its license, its size and whether it is:","maintained and compatible",["colorful","famous","old"],"Active support matters.")
H("688.d.6.J",3,"A simulator or emulator lets a developer test an app on:","a virtual phone on the computer",["a real paper phone","a printer","a TV only"],"Emulators mimic devices.")
H("688.d.6.J",4,"An emulator is useful because it lets a developer try many screen sizes without:","buying every device",["writing any code","using a computer","opening the app"],"Emulation saves cost.")
H("688.d.6.K",3,"Testing on a real device can reveal problems with:","touch response and battery use",["only spelling","only color names","nothing"],"Real hardware behaves differently.")
H("688.d.6.K",4,"Even after emulator testing passes, a team tests on actual phones because:","real hardware may behave differently",["emulators are illegal","phones never fail","testing is optional"],"Real devices show true performance.")

B.balance()
B.save(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "..", "batches", "cte_87.jsonl"))
